migrations/20261030120000_rls_once_per_query.sql:7:-- Inside a SECURITY DEFINER function such as file_access, that policy is bypassed, so the account filter here is the only per-user guard on grants.
---
1:-- New functions are not executable without a login. Grant anon explicitly where a client or policy needs it.
2:ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE postgres IN SCHEMA public REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTIONS FROM anon;
3:-- PUBLIC gets EXECUTE on new functions by default, and anon inherits it. Only a global default, not a per-schema one, can remove it.
4:ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE postgres REVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTIONS FROM public;
5:
6:-- Every role that calls our functions holds its own grant, so PUBLIC's grant only matters for anon.
7:-- Removing it makes existing functions match the ones created under the defaults above.
8:DO $$
9:DECLARE
10:    fn regprocedure;
11:BEGIN
12:    FOR fn IN
13:        SELECT p.oid::regprocedure FROM pg_catalog.pg_proc AS p
14:        WHERE p.pronamespace = 'public'::regnamespace
15:        AND p.proowner = 'postgres'::regrole
16:        AND NOT EXISTS (
17:            SELECT 1 FROM pg_catalog.pg_depend AS d
18:            WHERE d.classid = 'pg_catalog.pg_proc'::regclass AND d.objid = p.oid AND d.deptype = 'e'
19:        )
20:    LOOP
21:        EXECUTE format('REVOKE EXECUTE ON FUNCTION %s FROM PUBLIC', fn);
22:    END LOOP;
23:END;
24:$$;
25:
26:-- Only triggers call these. Postgres checks EXECUTE on a trigger function when the trigger is created, not when it fires.
27:REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.after_delete_space() FROM anon, authenticated;
28:REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.after_delete_update_fref() FROM anon, authenticated;
29:REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.after_insert_fref() FROM anon, authenticated;
30:REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.on_delete_local_reference() FROM anon, authenticated;

