  I ran `/code-review high main...HEAD` through the Skill tool. It reviewed commit e5697e83e on eng-2466-set-sensible-default-privileges-and-explicit-grants (PR #1610) against its merge base with main. It ran as a forked background agent, using static review only. I did not modify any file or check the findings myself. Its findings are below, verbatim, in the order it returned them.

  Several of them go over decisions you said were settled. Finding 1 is the CRUD default for authenticated on new objects. Finding 4 is space_insert_policy WITH CHECK (true). Finding 9 is service_role keeping writes on most views. Finding 3 partly touches production-only relations. I kept them because you asked for verbatim output; drop them as needed. On finding 9's last part: the review says `security_model.md` claims writes are revoked on every view, and that doc claim may still be worth checking.

  ```json
  [
    {
      "file": "packages/database/supabase/schemas/base.sql",
      "line": 31,
      "summary": "The defaults give `authenticated` INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE on every new view, so each new definer view stays writable unless someone remembers a per-view REVOKE. That is the same hole ENG-2368 and ENG-2359 patched one view at a time.",
      "failure_scenario": "A later migration adds a single-table definer view (for example `my_x AS SELECT ... FROM \"Concept\" WHERE ...`) and forgets the REVOKE. Any signed-in user can then INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE through it as the view owner, which bypasses Concept's RLS. Default privileges can't tell tables from views, so the fix belongs one level down: an event trigger on CREATE VIEW that revokes writes, or a guard test that fails when any view in public grants a write privilege."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/supabase/schemas/base.sql",
      "line": 28,
      "summary": "The per-schema `REVOKE ALL ON TABLES FROM anon, authenticated` only removes grants the image made inside the schema. If image 17.11.0.018 grants TRUNCATE, REFERENCES, TRIGGER and MAINTAIN through global defaults for postgres, those grants survive, and no test checks the privileges of a newly created table.",
      "failure_scenario": "On a fresh 018 build, a table created by a later migration could still give anon or authenticated TRUNCATE, which RLS does not limit, so a client can empty the table. The local test DB runs image 006, where every postgres default in public is per-schema, so this could not be checked against 018 here. A test that creates a table and reads its relacl would catch it."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/supabase/migrations/20261106130000_explicit_privileges.sql",
      "line": 39,
      "summary": "The DO loop grants `authenticated` SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE on every postgres-owned relation in public, without checking `relrowsecurity` and without limiting itself to the objects in the schema files.",
      "failure_scenario": "Production may hold a leftover table that was created by hand or never dropped, with authenticated access revoked or RLS disabled. After this migration every signed-in user can read and write all of its rows. Only `file_gc` and the views are re-restricted afterwards. The loop should skip, or at least raise on, tables where `relkind = 'r'` and `relrowsecurity` is false."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/supabase/schemas/account.sql",
      "line": 374,
      "summary": "`space_insert_policy` (WITH CHECK (true)) is left in place even though INSERT on Space is now revoked from every client role. The policy is dead code, and the advisor's `rls_policy_always_true` finding stays.",
      "failure_scenario": "A later change that grants INSERT on Space back, for example during the ENG-1837 grant work, silently brings back 'any signed-in user can create any Space and take its url', because the always-true policy is still there. Dropping the policy now would remove both the trap and the advisor finding."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/doc/security_model.md",
      "line": 129,
      "summary": "The advisor-findings entry still says `space_insert_policy` remains and 'Whether to remove it is still open'. It doesn't mention that this PR revokes INSERT on Space, which makes the policy unreachable.",
      "failure_scenario": "A reader comparing advisor output with this list keeps treating the always-true insert policy as a live open question, when the grant change has already settled it. The doc and the schema now disagree about how Space inserts are blocked."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/doc/security_model.future.md",
      "line": 110,
      "summary": "The future-model doc still says new plugin users must be able to insert their own Space through the always-true policy. That contradicts the new rule that only create-space can create spaces.",
      "failure_scenario": "Someone who implements the future model from this doc relies on the insert policy, but every insert fails with 'permission denied for table Space' because the privilege is revoked. The two security docs now give opposite rules."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/supabase/migrations/20261106130000_explicit_privileges.sql",
      "line": 110,
      "summary": "The DO loop grants EXECUTE to `authenticated` on every function, then nine trigger functions are revoked from a hand-written list. The loop could skip functions with `prorettype = 'trigger'::regtype` instead.",
      "failure_scenario": "If any trigger function exists in production but is missing from this list, it keeps EXECUTE for `authenticated` and nothing reports it. The list has to be kept in step with the schema files by hand, in both the migration and every schema file."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/supabase/migrations/20261106130000_explicit_privileges.sql",
      "line": 131,
      "summary": "`anon` is (re)granted EXECUTE on `concept_in_space`, `content_in_space` and `document_in_space`, but they call `in_space`, which anon cannot execute.",
      "failure_scenario": "Any anon path that reaches these functions, whether a direct RPC or a future policy, fails with 'permission denied for function in_space'. The grant does nothing useful, and it makes the anon function list in the migration and the schema files look larger than what anon can really use."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/supabase/migrations/20261106130000_explicit_privileges.sql",
      "line": 92,
      "summary": "Writes through views are handled two ways for `service_role`. It loses all write privileges on `accounts_in_my_partial_spaces` and `my_resource_grants`, but keeps INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE and the rest on the other nine definer views. The doc says write privileges are revoked on every view.",
      "failure_scenario": "Readers of security_model.md assume no role can write through a view, yet `service_role` still holds full write privileges on `my_concepts` and the other `my_*` views. Two ways of doing the same thing make the next view's grants a guess."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/features/step-definitions/stepdefs.ts",
      "line": 1858,
      "summary": "The new step for a logged-in client refused an insert repeats the body of the existing anonymous step line for line. The comment above the write-results step (\"a new Space is not visible to its creator, so a read-back would fail\") is also out of date, now that no client can insert a Space.",
      "failure_scenario": "Two copies of the same assertion drift apart when the error code or message check changes. A shared `assertInsertRefused(client, table, values)` helper, called by both steps, removes the copy. The stale comment points readers at a scenario (a client creating a Space) that can no longer happen."
    }
  ]
  ```

  The review reported no lost client-used privilege from `.from()` or `.rpc()` calls or the edge functions, and no SQL errors.

